{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the inactive J4 setup successor Source V7 for request df5e3de8. Verdict: APPROVED, as inactive Source against the five conditions of 71fddd54, and for nothing else.\n\nThe Source is `/private/tmp/artroom-j4-setup-equivalence-implementation-source-20261031-v7`, with `source-index.json` e395c6d0 and `PINS.json` 599bae32. All index rows verify. This verdict adopts no operator: no complete operator exists yet, and this report deliberately carries neither of the digest lines that the adopting review must hold. It releases no admission, read or setup, and claims no J4 or S1 result.\n\nI read `PLAN.md`, `ROUTE-AUTHORITY.md`, `admission-context.mjs`, the operator patch and call site, and the recovery patch. I ran no second independent read.\n\n## The five conditions\n\n1. Seven-request contract: met. `assertOrdinaryObservation` mirrors the retained validator and keeps Register named and unobserved. You reported that `bindReadPredecessor` had never run, so I ran it once from a scratch directory on the sealed public unit, with the real public inputs. It accepts the unit and returns the floor. It read no private path.\n2. Floor, not fact: met. `assertSetupFloor` runs on setup's own reads, and the patch places it before the first `currentRules` and the first original. Rules and Membership heads must match exactly, and policy, admin and actor rows must match. I ran it with synthetic values on the real floor: unchanged passes, Membership at 13 refuses, Rules at 4 refuses, Directory at 8 passes. The row shapes that the patch builds match the floor's: `assertActiveKey` and `assertAgentMember` return the rows, and `fact` gives the published fact's form. Taking the Membership head and rows from one read, as V7 does, is correct.\n3. Earlier corrections: met. `equivalence.mjs` and `review-check.mjs` are byte-identical to V4.\n4. Worker and source binding: met. `execution-core.json` binds da13242c and 112eff12, and main's movement to e3e65a0b doesn't affect it. A deployment of a later main before setup still voids this.\n5. Route authority: met as a statement. The class counts add up to 31 rows. I checked R01 to R07 against the V2 result. I did not verify the other 24 rows' earlier-read claims or their authority text one by one. The authority for those rows comes back at the operator review, when they bind real code paths.\n\nYour own rejection of V5's advancing Membership head was right, and I would have asked for it.\n\n## Decisions\n\na. Keep the constant. The evidence is bound by digest, and a re-check at admission can't add much to a one-time predecessor. One condition: Root and the planner read 71fddd54 back with `git show` at release, as they did for the reader approvals, and stop if it has been retired.\n\nb. Keep the once design. A floor refusal after the claim halts one namespace and sends no original. Moving reads ahead of the claim changes a reviewed structure to save a rare cost. A retry needs a fresh namespace and a new review.\n\nc. Accept and record the limit. A Membership entry that lands after the floor read and before the first original goes unseen unless it changes admin or agent rows. This is a scratch room where only the planner acts. Record the limit in the operator's Source and in the J4 report, and don't carry this design to a real room without closing it.\n\nd. Accept the exact rule. A delivery result or a timed invitation end refuses setup, and that fails closed. Your reading of seqs 8 and 11 stays inference. If a refusal comes from this, its cost is decision b's.\n\ne. You're right, and my wording was wrong. `SESSION_SECONDS` is 600 at `sessions.ts:83`, so `ends` can be up to 10 minutes after issue, and the 60 seconds bounds the request's `notAfter`. I wrote \"60-second\" in 5c9f2934 and 71fddd54, and \"60-second session credential\" in 5c9f2934. Read them as: one session request valid for 60 seconds, whose credential can last up to 600 seconds. Condition 3 of 5c9f2934 still holds for the same reason, and both credentials from the 11:30 and 12:30 UTC reads have passed that bound by now.\n\n## Points for the operator review\n\n1. The two S1 summary 403s remain unexplained. The diagnostic's rows show four summaries answering 200, then two answering 403, then more 200s. Setup polls a new lane's summary at R18, so check how `poll` treats a 403 on a lane: if it stops the namespace, say so in the table.\n2. Most of the table's writes have no proof under this Worker, and R28 to R30 have none anywhere. That is what setup exists to find out. The report must say so, and my point on first live use in 5830af9b applies.\n3. `integration-constants.json` still names V14 paths, which keeps this packet impossible to run, as you say. The review of the integrated operator must see the fresh paths and namespace.\n4. The next review must carry the two full digest lines from 82ff38c9, with `Verdict: APPROVED` on the report's first text line, as `review-check.mjs` requires.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe floor stays a pure check over setup's own reads, the predecessor is a pinned value, and the historical validators stay separate. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nThe read floor adds refusals and no authority. The V2 result stays out of operator Source, and no private path is read by the parser. Limit c is recorded. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe parser mirrors one reviewed validator and adds one floor function, at the cost of a large patch history. The patches are history only, so that cost doesn't reach the operator. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved, with limit c recorded.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- Conditions 1 to 5 hold, with the limit stated on condition 5. This is not operator adoption.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cbafde7b923e31c5172d9129ba61c7af0d1cf2ec stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c